 a plain index on `users.email` from being used. The planner has to scan every row in `users` and lower-case each email.\n   - Fix option 1: create an expression index with `CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));`. This works in Postgres, and MySQL 8.0.13+ supports it with double parentheses.\n   - Fix option 2: store emails normalized to lower case, or use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` in Postgres). Then compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly against a plain unique index.\n\n2. **No index on `orders.user_id`.**\n   - The join needs an index on `orders(user_id)`. Without one, fetching a user's orders means scanning the whole `orders` table. Postgres doesn't create this index automatically for a foreign key. InnoDB does.\n   - Fix: `CREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);`\n\n3. **`SELECT *`.**\n   - It pulls every column from both tables, including `users` columns repeated on every order row. That means more I/O and network traffic. It also prevents index-only scans.\n   - Both tables have an `id` column, and possibly other duplicated names. That can cause ambiguity or overwrite problems in client code.\n   - Fix: list only the columns you need.\n\n4. **No `LIMIT` or ordering, and the result size is unbounded.**\n   - If a user has many orders, the query returns all of them. If you only need recent orders, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT N`. Back it with a composite index such as `orders (user_id, created_at DESC)`.\n\nIf the email is unique, the lookup on `users` returns at most one row. After fixes 1 and 2, the plan should be an index seek on `users`, then an index range scan on `orders`. I only had the query text. I haven't seen the schema, existing indexes, or an `EXPLAIN` plan, so confirm with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on real data.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}